      en: 'In the nineteenth century British indigo planters forced Bengali peasants to grow indigo on their land, and built kuthis — factory-residences — to process the dye and control the trade. The indigo kuthi at Auliapur near Madaripur is one surviving trace of that era, and stories of the planters’ cruelty still circulate among local people.\n\nOnly ruins remain today: broken brick walls, pillars and fragments of structure half swallowed by trees and creepers. There is no museum or formal visitor centre, and little interpretation on site, so it helps to read a little about the indigo revolt of 1859–60 before you go.\n\nFor history lovers it is a quietly powerful place to reflect on the colonial economy that shaped rural Bengal. Visit in winter, when the undergrowth is thinner and the ground is dry enough to explore the ruins comfortably.',
